/* CCAR-F — Claude Certified Architect, Foundations.
   The "Go deeper" explanation under each lesson: the long-form prose its body,
   points and traps summarise — the mechanism, how the points connect, the
   decision rule and a worked example. Written from the CCAR-F study guide based on the official exam guide.

   Keyed by lesson ref. Each entry is a list of blocks: a string is a
   paragraph, { h } a subheading, { example, label? } a worked example and
   { code, label? } a code block. Text may carry <b>, <i> and <code>, as in the
   lesson files. data.js attaches each entry to its lesson as `more`; loaded
   before data.js. */

window.CCARF_DEEP = {

  "1.1": [
    { h: "What the loop is actually doing" },
    "The Messages API does one thing per call: it takes a complete conversation and returns the model's next turn. It does not remember the previous call, it does not run tools, and it does not know it is part of a loop. Everything that makes the system agentic lives in your code — the <code>while</code> loop, the tool execution, the growing <code>messages</code> list. Claude decides what to do next; your code carries that decision out and hands the result back.",
    "Each iteration has a fixed shape. You append the assistant's response to the history exactly as returned, including any text and every <code>tool_use</code> block, because the next request has to show the model what it asked for. You then run each requested tool and send one user-role message holding a <code>tool_result</code> block per <code>tool_use</code>, each referencing its <code>tool_use_id</code>. Drop the assistant turn, or send the result as loose text, and the model no longer has a coherent record of the exchange. Because the API is stateless, a history you forget to send is a history the model never had.",
    { h: "Why stop_reason is the only signal" },
    "<code>stop_reason</code> is a structured field the API sets to say why generation stopped. <code>\"tool_use\"</code> means the model is waiting on you: it has asked for a tool and cannot go on until it sees the result. <code>\"end_turn\"</code> means it considers the response complete. Those two values drive the loop; the other two need handling of their own. <code>\"max_tokens\"</code> means the output was cut off, so anything structured in it is probably incomplete — the fix is a larger limit or a smaller request, not treating the turn as finished. <code>\"stop_sequence\"</code> means one of your stop sequences fired, and what that implies is defined by your application.",
    "Every alternative signal is a guess at something <code>stop_reason</code> states outright. A phrase like \"task complete\" is prose: the model may write it before a final tool call, or finish without it. Checking whether the first content block is text fails because a response can open with a sentence of explanation and then carry a <code>tool_use</code> block. An iteration cap knows nothing about the task: set it low and it truncates legitimate work, set it high and it only fires after the agent has already wasted turns. Keep a cap as a guard against runaway loops, and treat hitting it as an error to investigate, never as completion.",
    { h: "Model-driven, with code where order matters" },
    "Inside the loop, Claude picks the next tool from the conversation and the results so far. That is the point of an agent. A hard-coded decision tree has to anticipate every path; the model can react to a result nobody predicted — an empty search, an unexpected error, a second issue buried in the customer's message. The exception is business logic that must run in a set order, such as verifying identity before a refund. There you keep the model-driven loop and constrain it with code (see 1.4 and 1.5) rather than scripting the whole flow.",
    "Loop questions on the exam are rarely about syntax. They describe a symptom — an agent that stops early, repeats a call, or hands the user a half-finished answer — and ask which control-flow decision caused it. Trace the symptom back to two things: what the loop read to decide whether to continue, and what it appended before the next call.",
    { example: "A support agent sometimes replies \"Let me check that order for you\" and the conversation ends with no lookup. The loop exits as soon as the response contains a text block. The proposals: raise <code>max_iterations</code> from 5 to 15; add \"never announce an action without performing it\" to the system prompt; exit only when <code>stop_reason</code> is <code>\"end_turn\"</code>; or search the text for phrases like \"let me check\" and continue if found. The failing responses carried a text block followed by a <code>tool_use</code> block, so their <code>stop_reason</code> was <code>\"tool_use\"</code> — the model was waiting for the lookup result. Switching the exit condition to <code>stop_reason</code> fixes the root cause. Raising the cap changes nothing, because the loop exits on the text check long before reaching it. The prompt edit targets model behaviour that was never wrong, and phrase-matching swaps one fragile text heuristic for another." }
  ],

  "1.2": [
    { h: "Why the topology is a star" },
    "A coordinator–subagent system is hub-and-spoke: subagents talk to the coordinator and to nothing else. The shape is deliberate. When every result and every failure passes through one agent, there is one place to observe what happened, one place that decides what each subagent is told, and one place that can recover when something breaks. Let subagents hand work directly to each other and you lose all three — a failure in the middle of a chain surfaces as an odd result at the end, with no agent positioned to notice it or reroute the work.",
    "Specialisation is the other half of the design. Each subagent gets a narrow job, a focused system prompt and only the tools that job needs, which improves its tool selection and keeps its context small. The coordinator carries the global picture — the original request, what has been delegated, what has come back — so no subagent has to. Because subagents start with isolated context (see 1.3), this division is not a style choice: the coordinator is the only agent that knows the whole task.",
    { h: "Decomposition sets the ceiling" },
    "The coordinator's first job, breaking the task down, caps the quality of everything after it. A subagent can only cover what it was assigned; it has no way to know that a sibling topic was left out. So when a final report is thorough but incomplete, read the delegation log before blaming the workers. If each subagent returned solid results on its slice, the gap was created at decomposition time, and better searching will not close it.",
    "Two decomposition errors recur. Too narrow: the slices fail to cover the request, and whole areas disappear without any error being raised. Overlapping: subagents get fuzzy boundaries and research the same ground, roughly doubling token cost without adding breadth. The fix for both is the same act — the coordinator partitions the space explicitly before delegating, by subtopic or by source type, so that coverage is complete and boundaries are clear. Deduplicating afterwards treats the symptom and still pays for the wasted work.",
    { h: "The refinement loop" },
    "A coordinator that aggregates and ships the first synthesis is a pipeline with extra steps. The stronger pattern evaluates the synthesis against the original request: which parts are thin, which claims lack support, which subtopics came back partial because a subagent failed. It then delegates targeted follow-up work and synthesises again. This is where structured error reporting pays off — a subagent that returns a partial failure with context (see 5.3) gives the coordinator what it needs to choose between retrying, rerouting and annotating the gap.",
    "On the exam, the coordinator is usually the answer to \"who owns this?\" Coverage, routing, retries and what each agent receives all sit with the hub. Options that push those decisions into a subagent, or bypass the hub for speed, trade away the observability the architecture exists to provide.",
    { example: "A research system asked about \"renewable energy adoption in Europe\" returns detailed sections on solar and wind but nothing on hydro, geothermal or policy. The logs show three subagents were assigned solar deployment, onshore wind and offshore wind, and each returned well-sourced results. The options: give the search subagent more tools; tell the synthesis agent to flag missing topics; have the coordinator partition the topic into complete, non-overlapping areas before delegating and check the synthesis for gaps; or let subagents message each other to coordinate coverage. The subagents did exactly what they were told, so their tools are not the problem. The synthesis agent never saw the original request's full scope, so it cannot reliably know what is missing. Peer-to-peer messaging breaks the hub-and-spoke model and still leaves nobody responsible for coverage. The defect is in the coordinator's decomposition, and so is the fix." }
  ],

  "1.3": [
    { h: "What a subagent starts with" },
    "A subagent spawned through the <code>Task</code> tool begins with its own system prompt, its allowed tools and the prompt the coordinator wrote for it — nothing more. It does not see the coordinator's conversation, the user's original message or any other subagent's output. That isolation is why subagents are worth having: exploration burns the subagent's context instead of the coordinator's, and only the result comes back. The price is that the coordinator has to write every fact the subagent needs into the <code>Task</code> prompt.",
    "The coordinator also needs permission to delegate. <code>Task</code> is a tool like any other, so it must appear in the coordinator's allowed tools; without it the coordinator can describe a delegation in prose but cannot perform one. Each subagent is configured separately through an <code>AgentDefinition</code>: the description says what it is for, the system prompt shapes how it works, and the allowed tools are cut to the minimum its job requires.",
    { h: "Writing the Task prompt" },
    "The classic failure is the one-line delegation — \"analyse the document\" — written by a coordinator that can see the document and forgets the subagent cannot. Pass prior agents' output in full rather than a paraphrase, because the coordinator's summary may drop exactly the detail the next agent needs. Put each piece in a labelled section, as in the lesson's example, so the subagent can separate the material it works on from the metadata about it: which source a finding came from, its date, the schema to return. Keeping those fields apart also preserves provenance when results flow back up.",
    "Full context means everything the task needs, not everything the coordinator knows. A subagent handed the entire history pays for irrelevant tokens and receives a blurrier job. Give it the specific task and the data that task depends on, and ask for structured results back rather than raw dumps, so the coordinator can aggregate without re-reading everything.",
    { h: "Parallel calls and goal-level prompts" },
    "When subtasks are independent, emit several <code>Task</code> calls in the same coordinator turn and they run concurrently. Issuing one per turn serialises work that had no reason to wait. Sequencing is right only when one subagent needs another's output — then the second runs after the first, with that output passed in its prompt.",
    "The coordinator's own prompt should state the goal and what a good result looks like — coverage, sourcing, format — rather than a numbered procedure. A procedure only handles the cases its author foresaw; goals and quality criteria let the coordinator choose which subagents to spawn and adapt when a result surprises it. <code>fork_session</code> sits in the same toolkit, for branching one shared context into two lines of exploration (see 1.7).",
    { example: "A coordinator has already run a web-search subagent that returned twelve sources with URLs and publication dates. It then spawns an analysis subagent with the prompt \"Compare the findings from the search and identify contradictions.\" The analysis comes back generic and cites nothing. The candidate fixes: give the analysis subagent the web-search tool so it can repeat the search; configure the two subagents to share memory; include all twelve results in the <code>Task</code> prompt in a labelled section, with source and date kept as separate fields, plus the output schema; or raise the analysis subagent's <code>max_tokens</code>. The subagent never received the results — \"the findings from the search\" referred to context only the coordinator held. Passing them explicitly, with metadata separated from content, is the fix. Re-running the search duplicates work and may return different sources, subagents do not share memory, and <code>max_tokens</code> caps the output rather than widening what the subagent can see." }
  ],

  "1.4": [
    { h: "Probabilistic and deterministic controls" },
    "An instruction in the system prompt changes how likely the model is to do something. It can raise compliance a long way, but it cannot make a step impossible to skip, and across thousands of conversations with unusual requests it will sometimes lose. That is acceptable for tone, formatting and soft preferences, where an occasional miss costs little. It is not acceptable for a step whose omission moves money to the wrong place, breaches a regulation or harms someone. Those rules have to live where the model cannot talk its way past them: in code.",
    "A programmatic precondition is the simplest such control. Your application tracks workflow state — has <code>get_customer</code> returned a verified ID in this conversation? — and refuses to execute downstream tools until it has. The model may still try <code>lookup_order</code> early; the call fails with a clear message that verification comes first, and the model recovers as it would from any tool error. The ordering has become a property of the system rather than a request to the model.",
    { h: "Choosing the control" },
    "The decision rule is about consequence, not frequency. Ask what happens in the one conversation in a hundred where the step is skipped. If the answer is a slightly off-tone reply, prompt guidance is proportionate. If it is a refund to the wrong account, a prompt is the wrong layer however carefully it is worded, and few-shot examples are the same kind of control with the same ceiling. Exam options that \"strengthen\", \"emphasise\" or \"clarify\" a mandatory rule are telling you they remain probabilistic.",
    "Enforcement does not replace model judgement. The model still decides which tools to call and how to respond; code only closes the paths that must never be taken out of order. This is also why a precondition beats a routing classifier for a sequencing problem: a classifier controls which tools are available for a type of request, not the order in which they run.",
    { h: "Multi-part requests and the handoff" },
    "Customers often bundle issues — a refund on one order, an address change on another. Track each as a separate item with its own parameters and status, so that resolving one cannot quietly stand in for all of them and details do not bleed from one order into another. Before the conversation closes, every item is either resolved or explicitly handed off.",
    "When the agent escalates, the handoff object is all the human receives. It must stand alone: who the customer is, which order, what went wrong and why, what the agent has already verified and tried, what it recommends, and why it is escalating. Leave out the actions taken and the human repeats them; leave out the reason and they cannot tell a policy gap from a customer who asked for a manager. When to escalate in the first place is covered in 5.2.",
    { example: "An insurance claims agent must confirm a policy is active before calling <code>approve_payout</code>. An audit finds eleven payouts last quarter on lapsed policies; each time, the claimant described their policy in detail and the agent treated that as confirmation. The proposals: a capitalised line in the system prompt saying <code>check_policy_status</code> is mandatory; five few-shot examples showing the check; a nightly report of payouts on lapsed policies; or a precondition in the tool layer that rejects <code>approve_payout</code> unless <code>check_policy_status</code> returned active earlier in the session. Only the precondition guarantees the order. The prompt line and the examples lower the failure rate but leave the failure possible, which a financial control cannot accept. The nightly report is useful monitoring, but it finds the payout after the money has gone." }
  ],

  "1.5": [
    { h: "Where hooks sit" },
    "A hook is your code, registered to run at a fixed point in the agent lifecycle. Two points carry most of the exam's weight. <code>PreToolUse</code> runs after the model has decided to call a tool but before the call executes, so the hook sees the tool name and arguments and can let the call through, block it or redirect it. <code>PostToolUse</code> runs after the tool returns but before the result reaches the model, so the hook can reshape what the model is about to read. Neither depends on the model choosing to run it — it fires every time its event occurs, which is what makes a hook a control rather than a suggestion.",
    "A hook configuration has three parts: the event (the lifecycle point), a matcher (which tools or events trigger it) and the handler that runs. Keep the matcher tight. A refund-limit hook should match <code>process_refund</code>, not inspect every call the agent makes.",
    { h: "PostToolUse: normalising what the model sees" },
    "An agent that combines several MCP servers inherits their inconsistencies: one returns Unix timestamps, another \"Mar 5, 2025\", a third numeric status codes where 1 means pending. The model can often interpret these, but often is the problem — one misread date becomes a wrong eligibility decision. A <code>PostToolUse</code> hook converts everything to one convention before the model reads it. It works on third-party servers you cannot modify, it lives in one place rather than being repeated in each tool, and it is deterministic, unlike format notes in the system prompt.",
    "The same hook protects the context window. Tool results pile up over a long conversation, and a 40-field record where the task uses five wastes most of the space it occupies on every later turn. Trimming to the relevant fields before the result lands keeps the model focused. Trim deliberately: drop what the task never uses, not what a later step might need.",
    { h: "PreToolUse: enforcing policy before it happens" },
    "Rules that must hold every time belong in a pre-call hook. It inspects the outgoing call, compares it with the policy — the refund amount against the $500 limit, say — and blocks it, returning a message that routes the case to escalation instead. The model then sees why the action did not happen and can carry on with the conversation properly. The contrast with prompting is exact: an instruction gives high but probabilistic compliance, the hook gives a guarantee.",
    "The exam pairs hooks with plausible alternatives. A <code>normalize_data</code> tool still depends on the model remembering to call it. A wrapper per tool works but multiplies code and misses the server you forgot. A post-hoc audit catches the breach after it has happened. When the requirement says always, never, or names a compliance threshold, look for the hook.",
    { example: "An operations agent can call <code>restart_service</code> on production hosts through an MCP server. Policy says production restarts outside the change window need on-call approval. After one unapproved restart at 3 a.m., the team considers: adding the change-window rule to the system prompt; giving the agent a <code>get_change_window</code> tool and telling it to check first; a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook matching <code>restart_service</code> that checks the target and the time and blocks out-of-window production restarts with a message telling the agent to request approval; or a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook that logs every restart for review. Only the <code>PreToolUse</code> hook stops the action before it runs, whatever the model decided. The prompt rule and the extra tool both rely on the model following them. The <code>PostToolUse</code> hook fires after the restart has already happened, so it records the breach instead of preventing it." }
  ],

  "1.6": [
    { h: "The deciding question" },
    "Before you choose a shape, ask one thing: can you write down every step before the work starts? If you can, a fixed pipeline — prompt chaining — turns the task into a sequence of focused calls, each doing one job with a narrow prompt and producing output the next step consumes. Because the steps never change, output is stable from run to run, and each stage can be tested and tuned on its own. Document processing, templated reviews and file migrations fit this shape.",
    "If the steps depend on what you find, a pipeline has nowhere to put the discovery. Dynamic adaptive decomposition starts by mapping the ground — Glob and Grep across the codebase, for example — then builds a prioritised plan from what the map shows and revises it as new facts appear. In the lesson's legacy-test example, nobody could have known about the external API dependency at the start. A fixed plan would have produced tests that fail against the real service; the adaptive plan inserts a mock step as soon as the dependency surfaces.",
    { h: "Why chaining beats one big prompt" },
    "Chaining is not only about predictability. Give a model many files at once and its attention spreads unevenly: some files get careful analysis, others a line of generic comment, and the same pattern may be flagged in one file and passed in another. One pass per file gives every file the same depth of attention. A separate integration pass then looks only at what lies between files — types that disagree across a boundary, circular dependencies, a contract changed on one side and not the other.",
    "Neither pass can do the other's job. A per-file pass cannot see a cross-file issue because it never holds both files, and a single combined pass dilutes both kinds of analysis. The problem is how evenly the model attends across a large input, not whether the input fits, so the structural fix is to give each call less to hold.",
    { h: "Mixing the two" },
    "Real systems combine the shapes. An open-ended investigation may begin adaptively and, once the ground is mapped, hand a well-defined batch of work to a fixed chain. A coordinator (see 1.2) typically decomposes dynamically, while the per-file review it delegates runs as a pipeline. What the exam tests is a mismatch: a rigid pipeline imposed on exploration, which cannot react to what it discovers, or free-form adaptive planning applied to a repeatable job, which gives up reproducibility for flexibility the task never needed.",
    { example: "A team asks an agent to find out why checkout latency doubled after last month's release. One engineer proposes a five-step pipeline: read the release notes, profile the checkout endpoint, list slow queries, propose index changes, write a report. Another proposes that the agent first map what changed — diff the release, trace the checkout path, list new dependencies — and then decide what to investigate from what it finds. This is an open-ended investigation: the cause could be a query, a new external call, a cache change or a configuration flag, and the pipeline has assumed the answer is database indexes before any evidence exists. The adaptive approach is correct. The pipeline is tempting because it looks thorough and reproducible, but reproducibility only helps when the steps are known in advance." }
  ],

  "1.7": [
    { h: "What a session carries" },
    "A session is a saved conversation: every message, every tool call and every tool result up to the moment you stopped. <code>claude --resume</code> with a session name reloads that history, so a long investigation can continue without re-running its discovery. Naming sessions after the work they hold — <code>investigation-auth-bug</code> rather than an opaque ID — is what makes resuming practical across days.",
    "The saved history is a snapshot, not a live view. When the agent read a file last week, the contents it saw were stored in the session as a tool result. Resume after a teammate refactors that file and the agent still holds the old version, with nothing marking it as out of date. It will reason confidently about code that no longer exists.",
    { h: "Resume, inform or start fresh" },
    "The choice turns on how much of the saved context is still true. If nothing relevant has changed, resume. If a few known files have changed, resume and tell the agent which ones, so it re-reads them instead of trusting its stored results. If a lot has changed, or the session has run long enough that its context has degraded, start a new session seeded with a structured summary: what was established, where it lives, what remains open. A short, accurate summary is a better foundation than a long, partly stale history, and findings written to a scratchpad file during the work make that summary cheap to produce (see 5.4).",
    { h: "Forking" },
    "<code>fork_session</code> answers a different question — not whether to continue, but how to try two things from the same starting point. Both branches inherit the full context up to the fork and then diverge independently, so neither approach contaminates the other and neither has to repeat the shared investigation. Use it to compare designs, such as a Redux against a Context API approach, or to test two debugging hypotheses side by side.",
    "Forking refreshes nothing. A fork of a stale session inherits the stale tool results along with everything else, so the resume-or-restart judgement comes first and the fork comes after.",
    { example: "Ten days ago you ran a long session mapping the payment module ahead of a refactor. Since then another team has merged changes to four files in that module, and the session history is very long. You now want to design the refactor. The options: resume and carry on; resume and give the model a larger context window; fork the old session so the original stays intact; or start a new session seeded with a structured summary of the earlier findings, listing the four changed files to re-read. With several files changed and a long, ageing history, the fresh session with a summary is the most reliable. Resuming as-is builds on stale results, a bigger window keeps every stale result and adds nothing, and forking copies the stale context into a second branch instead of refreshing it. Had only one small file changed, resuming and flagging that file would have been the better call." }
  ],

  "2.1": [
    { h: "Why the description does the routing" },
    "Claude never runs a tool and never sees its implementation. When it decides which tool to call, the only evidence it has is what you sent: the name, the description and the input schema of every tool in the request, plus the conversation so far. If two tools read the same on paper, the model has no basis for telling them apart, and the misrouting you see in the logs is the specification failing, not the model.",
    "That is why the first fix for a selection problem is almost always the description itself. It addresses the root cause, it costs nothing at runtime, and nothing else in the system has to change. Few-shot examples of correct routing, a classifier in front of the model or merging the tools all add machinery around a gap you have not closed.",
    { h: "What a selecting description contains" },
    "Read the <code>get_customer</code> example above sentence by sentence and each one does a job. The first states the purpose. The second says what comes back, so the model can judge whether calling the tool would actually answer the question in front of it. <i>Use this BEFORE lookup_order</i> draws the boundary against the neighbouring tool and fixes the order. The format example (<code>user@domain.com</code>, a numeric <code>customer_id</code>) cuts malformed inputs. Write it the way you would brief a new colleague, and name parameters so they cannot be misread — <code>customer_id</code>, not <code>customer</code>.",
    "The sentence most often missing is the boundary. A useful test: put the descriptions of two similar tools side by side and ask whether a realistic request could match both. If it could, each description needs a line saying what it is for and what it is not for, naming the alternative.",
    { h: "When the words are not enough" },
    "Sometimes the overlap is in the names as much as the prose. <code>analyze_content</code> and <code>analyze_document</code> will stay confusable however carefully you word them, because the name is part of what the model reads. Renaming the web tool to <code>extract_web_results</code> and describing it around web search and URLs removes the overlap at its source. The opposite problem is one tool doing several jobs: a long list of optional parameters whose valid combinations depend on each other is several operations wearing one name, and splitting it into narrow tools with clear input and output contracts makes wrong combinations impossible rather than merely documented.",
    "Two side effects come from outside the tool definitions. The model reads the system prompt when choosing tools, so a blanket instruction such as \"always verify the customer\" becomes an association that pulls <code>get_customer</code> into turns that never needed it; scope the instruction to the situations that actually require it. And where an MCP tool overlaps a built-in such as Grep, the agent tends to reach for the built-in. There is no precedence setting to change — the MCP description has to say what only it can provide.",
    { example: "A support agent has <code>search_kb</code> (\"Searches knowledge\") and <code>search_tickets</code> (\"Searches support information\"). Questions like \"has anyone reported this checkout bug before?\" go to the knowledge base, which returns product articles, and the agent tells the customer there is no known issue. The tempting fix is to merge both into one <code>search</code> tool with a <code>source</code> parameter, but that only moves the same ambiguity into a parameter the model still has to choose. Few-shot examples would steer the common phrasings and miss the rest. The right first step is to rewrite both descriptions: <code>search_tickets</code> searches past customer tickets, returns ticket ID, status and date, and is the tool for checking whether an issue has already been reported — not for product documentation, which is <code>search_kb</code>. Now the request matches exactly one description." }
  ],

  "2.2": [
    { h: "What the flag says and what it leaves out" },
    "<code>isError: true</code> tells the agent one thing: the call did not succeed. Everything the agent does next — wait and retry, change the input, tell the user, hand over to a human — depends on what sits beside the flag. The model can recover from an error it can read. Given only \"Operation failed\", it has to guess, and the usual guess is to keep probing: the same call again, then a variation, until the step or token budget runs out.",
    "The fields in the good example each answer a question the agent would otherwise have to guess. <code>errorCategory</code> and <code>isRetryable</code> decide whether another attempt can help at all. The message says what went wrong in terms the agent can relay or act on. <code>attempted_query</code> lets it vary the next attempt instead of repeating it, and <code>partial_results</code> keeps whatever was retrieved before the failure, so completed work is not thrown away.",
    { h: "Four categories, four different next moves" },
    "The categories matter because each one maps to a different action, and the wrong action is expensive in both directions. Retrying a validation error unchanged fails identically every time; the message has to name the invalid field, ideally with a valid example, so the retry is a corrected request. A business error, such as a refund above the policy threshold, is not a malfunction at all: mark it <code>isRetryable: false</code>, give a clear explanation the agent can pass to the user, and point to the alternative. A permission error will not fix itself on retry either, so it goes to escalation. Only transient failures — timeouts, 503s, dropped connections — are worth retrying as they are, with backoff.",
    { h: "Empty is not the same as failed" },
    "The most damaging error is the one that does not look like an error. A search tool that catches a timeout and returns an empty result with <code>isError: false</code> tells the agent there were no matches, and that becomes a finding in the final answer. \"No patents exist\" and \"the patent database was unreachable\" are different facts about the world. The reverse mistake is costly too: flagging a genuine zero-result search as an error sends the agent into retries for an answer it already has.",
    { h: "Recover where the knowledge is" },
    "The component closest to the failure knows most about it. A tool or subagent that hits a timeout should retry it locally — once or twice, with backoff — and only then propagate, carrying the category, the query and any partial results so the coordinator can choose between a modified retry, using what came back, or annotating a gap (see 5.3). Unbounded retries inside a subagent burn latency, and aborting the whole workflow over one failed source discards every result that did succeed.",
    { example: "A refund tool rejects a 900 dollar refund because it exceeds the limit the agent is allowed to approve. It returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"content\": \"Request failed\"}</code>, so the agent retries three times and then tells the customer there is a system problem. The tempting fix is a retry cap, which only makes the agent give up sooner with the same wrong explanation. The real defect is that a policy decision is dressed as a malfunction. Return <code>errorCategory: \"business\"</code>, <code>isRetryable: false</code> and a message stating that refunds above the agent's limit need human approval. The agent now stops retrying, explains the situation accurately and offers the alternative — routing the request for approval — which is what the category exists to trigger." }
  ],

  "2.3": [
    { h: "Why more tools means worse choices" },
    "Every tool definition is sent with every request, costs input tokens each time, and is one more option the model weighs before each call. Selection is a comparison across the whole set, so each tool you add makes every decision a little harder — more near-matches, more overlapping descriptions, more chances to pick the plausible wrong one. An agent with eighteen tools is not eighteen times as capable as one with four; it is measurably less reliable at picking any of them. Connecting a thirty-tool MCP server to an agent that needed two of them degrades its original tasks for exactly this reason.",
    "There is a second effect beyond confusion. A tool in the list reads as permission to use it. An agent holding tools outside its specialisation will eventually find a reason to call them, and then it is doing another agent's job badly. Scoping the toolset per role — the four or five tools the role needs, plus a small set of shared utilities — is how you keep each agent inside its job.",
    { h: "Constrain the tool rather than argue with the model" },
    "When an agent misuses a broad capability, the durable fix is at the interface. A document agent given <code>fetch_url</code> so it could download files will eventually fetch search result pages and start doing web research. Replacing it with <code>load_document</code>, which only accepts URLs that point at documents, makes the unwanted behaviour impossible; a line in the prompt only discourages it, and a domain blocklist chases hostnames forever.",
    "The exam's least-privilege pair works the same way. A synthesis agent that needs quick fact checks should get one narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool, not the research agent's whole search toolset. The narrow tool covers the frequent simple case without a round trip, and anything more involved still goes back through the coordinator, so the division of roles survives.",
    { h: "What each tool_choice setting guarantees" },
    "<code>tool_choice</code> changes what the model is allowed to do on this request, which is why it is the answer whenever a question asks for a guarantee. <code>auto</code> is the default: call a tool or answer in text. <code>any</code> requires some tool call but leaves the choice of tool to the model. <code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": ...}</code> requires that one tool. <code>none</code> forbids tool calls. The decision rule follows from what you need to guarantee: structured output with several candidate schemas is <code>any</code>; a specific first step or a fixed order is the named tool; everything else is <code>auto</code>. A system prompt saying \"always call a tool\" guarantees nothing, and leaving a single tool in the list still permits a text answer.",
    { example: "An enrichment pipeline must run <code>extract_metadata</code> before any of its three enrichment tools, but on some documents the model jumps straight to enrichment and the later steps work from guesses. Options: a system prompt line saying to extract metadata first; <code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}</code>; forcing <code>extract_metadata</code> by name on the first request; removing the enrichment tools. <code>any</code> is the tempting answer because it forces a tool call, but it does not decide which tool, and the model is already calling tools — just the wrong one first. The prompt line is probabilistic, and removing the enrichment tools breaks the rest of the pipeline. Forcing the named tool on the first request guarantees the order, and the following requests can let the model choose among the enrichment tools as normal." }
  ],

  "2.4": [
    { h: "What a server brings with it" },
    "An MCP server is a process that speaks the Model Context Protocol and exposes capabilities to a client such as Claude Code or your own agent. A local server is typically launched by the client from a command and arguments — the <code>npx</code> entry in the <code>.mcp.json</code> example — and talks over standard input and output; a remote one is reached over HTTP and needs real authentication. Either way, the client connects and discovers what the server offers without you listing it.",
    "The three primitives have different jobs. Tools are actions the agent calls. Resources are data the agent reads for context without acting. Prompts are templates for common tasks. Because every tool from every connected server is discovered on connection and available at the same time, connecting a server is not a neutral act: its tools join every selection the agent makes, and their descriptions decide how they get used (see 2.1 and 2.3).",
    { h: "Scope follows the audience" },
    "The scope question is always \"who needs this server?\" A server the whole team relies on goes in the project's <code>.mcp.json</code> at the repository root, committed, so everyone who clones gets the same configuration and changes go through review like code. A server you are trying out, or one only you use, goes in <code>~/.claude.json</code>, where it never touches the repository. Configuring a team server per person means every copy drifts; putting an experiment in the project file forces it on everyone.",
    "Committing the configuration must not mean committing the credential. <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code> in the <code>env</code> block is substituted from each developer's own environment when the server starts, so the file is one shared source of truth while every developer's token stays their own. A placeholder token in the file is a committed value waiting to be misused; an inline real token is a leak.",
    { h: "Resources save exploration" },
    "Without a resource, an agent working against an unfamiliar system spends its first several calls finding out what exists: listing tables, describing columns, paging through a task list. A resource such as a database schema, a task catalogue or an API reference hands it that map before it acts, so the calls it does make are aimed. If the logs show a run of orientation calls at the start of every task, that is the signal a resource is missing.",
    { h: "Reuse before you build" },
    "For standard integrations such as GitHub, Jira or Slack, an existing community server already handles the API, pagination and authentication, and maintaining your own copy buys nothing. Build a custom server for workflows genuinely specific to your team. MCP earns its keep through reuse across clients; standing up a server so one application can call one internal action adds a protocol, a deployment and an auth surface with nothing to reuse.",
    { example: "An analytics agent queries an internal warehouse through a custom MCP server with <code>list_tables</code>, <code>describe_table</code> and <code>run_query</code>. Every task begins with six to ten listing and describing calls before the first query, and on long tasks the agent runs out of steps. The tempting fix is to tell the agent in the prompt to explore less, but that removes the calls without giving it the knowledge they were fetching, so its queries get worse. The right change is to expose the warehouse schema as an MCP resource. The agent reads the map up front, goes straight to <code>run_query</code> with the right table and column names, and the exploratory calls disappear because they are no longer needed." }
  ],

  "2.5": [
    { h: "One question per tool" },
    "Each built-in tool answers a different question, and most mistakes come from asking the right question of the wrong tool. Glob answers \"which files have names like this?\" — it matches paths and never opens a file. Grep answers \"where does this text appear?\" — it searches contents. So \"find every test file\" is Glob on <code>**/*.test.tsx</code>, while \"find every caller of <code>processRefund</code>\" is Grep for the name. Read brings a whole file into context, Write creates a file or replaces one entirely, Edit changes one precise snippet, and Bash runs shell commands such as git, npm, the test suite or a build.",
    { h: "Why Edit needs a unique match" },
    "Edit locates its target by matching the text you give it, so the text is the address. If that snippet appears three times, there is no way to tell which occurrence you meant, and Edit fails rather than guess. The fallback is to Read the whole file, make the change on the full content, and Write the result back. Dropping to Bash and <code>sed</code> is the tempting shortcut, but it trades a tool that refused an ambiguous change for one that will happily apply it to every occurrence.",
    { h: "Investigate incrementally" },
    "Reading every file in a directory up front fills the context with material that mostly turns out to be irrelevant, and it pushes out the parts you actually need (see 5.4). The incremental pattern lets each result decide the next step: Grep for the entry point, Read the file it lands in, Grep for the places that use it, Read those consumers, and repeat until the picture is complete. You read a handful of files instead of hundreds, and every one of them is there for a reason.",
    "Wrapper modules are where this pays off. Codebases often re-export or wrap a function under another name, so a Grep for the original finds only the wrapper and one or two direct callers. Reading the wrapper tells you the new name; a second Grep for that name finds the real consumers. Missing the second hop is how an impact analysis reports three call sites when there are thirty.",
    { example: "You are asked what would break if the signature of <code>formatCurrency</code> changes, in a codebase you have not seen before. The tempting approach is to Glob <code>**/*.ts</code> and Read everything, which exhausts context long before it finishes; a Glob for files named <code>*currency*</code> is also wrong, because it finds file names, not usages. The right path is Grep for <code>formatCurrency</code>, which finds its definition and a file <code>utils/money.ts</code>. Reading that file shows it wraps the function as <code>formatPrice</code>. A second Grep for <code>formatPrice</code> finds the forty components that actually depend on it, and you Read only the ones whose usage is not obvious from the match." }
  ],

  "3.1": [
    { h: "Where you put it decides who gets it" },
    "CLAUDE.md is loaded at the start of every Claude Code session, so anything in it applies without anyone having to ask. The three levels answer one question: who should receive this instruction? Only you — your working style, your preferred verbosity — goes in <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, which lives on your machine and never enters version control. Everyone working on the project — coding and testing standards, architectural decisions — goes in the project file, committed, so it reaches every current and future contributor on clone. Conventions that only matter in one part of the tree go in a CLAUDE.md in that directory, which applies when Claude works with files there.",
    "The levels add up rather than override. All the files that apply are loaded together, ordered from broadest scope to most specific, so a directory file layers local detail on top of the project and user files instead of replacing them. That is the opposite of how settings files behave, and it means a convention cannot be switched off for one directory by omitting it there.",
    { h: "Keeping it modular" },
    "A single CLAUDE.md that has grown to hundreds of lines is hard to find things in and hard to review. Two mechanisms break it up. An <code>@path</code> import pulls another file in where it is referenced, so each package's CLAUDE.md can include just the standards relevant to it without copying them. Relative paths resolve against the file that contains the import, not the directory you launched from, and imports nest at most five levels deep. The <code>.claude/rules/</code> directory splits instructions into topic files instead — testing, API conventions, deployment — each owned and updated separately.",
    "Neither mechanism changes when the content loads: an imported file loads with the file that imports it, and a rule without path frontmatter loads alongside CLAUDE.md. If something should only load for certain files, that is a path-scoped rule (see 3.3); if it should only load for a certain task, it is a skill (see 3.2).",
    { h: "Why it belongs in the file, not the chat" },
    "An instruction typed into the conversation lasts for that conversation and may not survive compaction; the project-root CLAUDE.md is re-read from disk and comes back. <code>/memory</code> opens CLAUDE.md for editing, which is the quick way to turn something you keep repeating into something that persists. Remember too that CLAUDE.md is a Claude Code feature: a service calling the API directly receives only the system prompt it sends, so a convention there never reaches it.",
    { example: "In a monorepo, the payments package has strict rules — money as integer minor units, every state change audited — and someone put them in the root CLAUDE.md. Now frontend work is being steered by payments conventions, and the file has grown hard to maintain. Moving the rules to user-level CLAUDE.md is tempting because it gets them out of everyone's way, but it gets them out of everyone's way entirely: only one person would receive them. A skill is wrong because these rules must apply automatically, not when someone remembers to invoke them. The conventions are genuinely local to one directory, so a CLAUDE.md inside <code>packages/payments/</code> is the fit. It is committed, it applies whenever Claude works on payments files, and it stays out of the context for everything else." }
  ],

  "3.2": [
    { h: "Commands and skills are now one mechanism" },
    "A command file at <code>.claude/commands/review.md</code> and a skill at <code>.claude/skills/review/SKILL.md</code> both create <code>/review</code>. Current Claude Code has unified the two: skills are the recommended form, and command files still work, which is why the exam guide refers to <code>.claude/commands/</code>. What the skill format adds is a directory for supporting files and frontmatter that controls how the skill runs. A skill can also be loaded automatically when its description matches the task, and until then only its name and description sit in context, so an occasionally needed procedure costs almost nothing when unused.",
    "Location works exactly as it does for CLAUDE.md. The project's <code>.claude/</code> directory is committed, so a command there reaches the whole team on clone and everyone runs the same workflow. <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> and <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> are personal. A personal variant of a team skill belongs in your own directory under a different name, so experimenting with it never changes what your teammates run.",
    { h: "Frontmatter turns instructions into guarantees" },
    "Each frontmatter key fixes a specific failure that instructions inside the skill cannot. <code>context: fork</code> runs the skill in an isolated subagent: its file reads, intermediate reasoning and long output stay in that context and only the result comes back. Without it, a deep analysis leaves the main session sluggish and cluttered, and a brainstorming skill leaves rejected approaches in the history for Claude to keep referring back to. <code>allowed-tools</code> limits what the skill can touch — a skill that only needs Read, Grep and Glob cannot delete a file however it interprets its instructions. <code>argument-hint</code> prompts for a required parameter when someone invokes the skill bare, instead of letting it run on a guess.",
    { h: "Skill, CLAUDE.md or rule" },
    "The choice turns on when the content is needed. Standards that must apply to every session belong in CLAUDE.md, because it always loads. Procedures that matter only during a particular task — a review checklist, deployment steps, a migration procedure — belong in skills, loaded when that task comes up. Path-scoped rules key off the file being edited (see 3.3), so they are the wrong tool when the same files host several different tasks. A CLAUDE.md that has grown by absorbing checklists and runbooks is usually several skills waiting to be extracted.",
    { example: "A tech lead wrote a <code>/release-notes</code> command that reads every commit since the last tag and drafts notes. It lives in her <code>~/.claude/commands/</code>, teammates want it, and whoever runs it finds the session afterwards buried under dozens of commit summaries. Asking teammates to copy the file is tempting, but every copy then drifts. Pasting the procedure into CLAUDE.md is wrong because it would load in every session to serve a task done once a sprint. The fix is a project skill at <code>.claude/skills/release-notes/SKILL.md</code> with <code>context: fork</code>: it arrives with the repository, and the commit-by-commit reading happens in an isolated context that hands back only the draft." }
  ],

  "3.3": [
    { h: "How a path-scoped rule loads" },
    "A file in <code>.claude/rules/</code> with no frontmatter is simply part of the always-loaded instructions, split out by topic. Add a <code>paths</code> array of glob patterns and it becomes conditional: Claude Code loads it only when it is working on a file that matches one of the patterns. Your API conventions stop costing context on a stylesheet fix, and your test conventions appear exactly when a test file is opened, however large the rest of the instruction set grows.",
    "The deeper benefit is determinism. A glob either matches the file being edited or it does not. A section buried in a long CLAUDE.md applies only if the model notices that it applies, which is the unreliable step you are trying to remove. \"Claude will work out which section is relevant\" is the argument the rule mechanism answers.",
    { h: "Patterns follow file type, not folders" },
    "The <code>**</code> segment matches any depth, so <code>**/*.test.tsx</code> reaches every co-located test file in the codebase — beside components, beside hooks, in packages that do not exist yet. That is something no directory-based mechanism can do when tests live next to the code they cover. A pattern can equally be tied to a tree, such as <code>terraform/**/*</code>, and one rule can list several patterns, as the test rule above does for <code>.test.tsx</code> and <code>.test.ts</code>.",
    { h: "Choosing between rules, directory CLAUDE.md and skills" },
    "Ask what the convention is attached to. If it follows a kind of file spread across many directories — tests, migrations, generated clients — a path-scoped rule is the fit, because one file covers every location and new directories are covered automatically. If it is tied to one directory and needed nowhere else, a CLAUDE.md in that directory is simpler and sits beside the code it governs. If it follows a task rather than a file — a review checklist, a release procedure — neither works, because both load for every task on those files; that is a skill (see 3.2).",
    { example: "Database migrations live in <code>services/*/migrations/</code> across twelve services, and every migration must be reversible and must not mix data changes into schema changes. The options: a CLAUDE.md in each of the twelve migrations directories; a section in the root CLAUDE.md; a <code>/migration</code> skill; or a rule with <code>paths: [\"services/*/migrations/**/*\"]</code>. Twelve directory files is the tempting answer because each is local, but that is twelve copies to keep in step, and the thirteenth service will be created without one. The root section loads for every task and relies on the model spotting that it applies, and the skill only helps when someone remembers to invoke it. The path-scoped rule is one file, loads exactly when a migration is being edited, and covers new services the day they are created." }
  ],

  "3.4": [
    { h: "What plan mode changes" },
    "Plan mode is a permission mode in which Claude can investigate but not act. It explores with Read, Grep and Glob, works out an approach, and presents an implementation plan; nothing is edited until you approve it. The value is not caution for its own sake. It moves the expensive decisions — service boundaries, which library, what to change in what order — to the point where changing your mind costs a conversation rather than a pile of edits to undo.",
    { h: "The decision rule" },
    "Ask two questions: is there more than one plausible way to do this, and how far does the change reach? A single-file fix with a clear stack trace, or one validation check in a well-understood place, has one obvious approach and a small blast radius, so planning adds a round trip and catches nothing. A migration touching dozens of files, a monolith split with open questions about boundaries, a choice between integration styles, or any change to a codebase you do not yet understand has several viable paths and a high cost of picking wrongly. That is plan mode.",
    "Two distractors recur. \"Direct execution with a very detailed instruction\" assumes you already know the structure you are trying to discover. \"Start direct and switch to plan mode if it gets hard\" is reactive: by the time it is visibly hard, the rework has already happened.",
    { h: "Plan, approve, execute" },
    "The usual right answer is the combination. Plan mode handles investigation and design, you review and approve the plan, and then execution proceeds directly against it. Execution is no longer exploratory — the decisions are made and the work is carrying them out, which is exactly the shape direct execution suits.",
    { h: "Keeping discovery out of the main context" },
    "Discovery on a large codebase is verbose: hundreds of search hits and file reads, most of which matter only long enough to be summarised. Done in the main session, it fills the context window before the design and implementation phases begin. The Explore subagent runs that discovery in its own context and returns a summary, so the main session keeps its room for the work that follows. Compacting afterwards is the weaker alternative — the tokens have already been spent, and summarisation can lose exact details.",
    { example: "Two tickets arrive. The first: a null reference in <code>parseHeader</code>, with a stack trace pointing at one line of one file. The second: move sixty files from version 2 of the HTTP client to version 3, whose retry and timeout behaviour changed in ways the release notes only partly describe. Running both in plan mode \"to be safe\" is the tempting blanket answer, but for the first ticket it adds an approval round trip to a fix whose approach is already obvious — direct execution is right. The second has a wide blast radius and real decisions about how to map the old behaviour onto the new, so it goes to plan mode, with an Explore subagent cataloguing the call sites so the main session receives a summary rather than sixty files of search output. Once the plan is approved, the migration itself runs directly." }
  ],

  "3.5": [
    { h: "Show the transformation, do not describe it" },
    "A description of a transformation leaves the edge cases to interpretation. \"Normalise the names to title case\" says nothing about \"O'Brien\", \"van der Berg\" or \"McDonald\", and each reasonable reading gives a different function. Two or three concrete input and output pairs settle those questions at once, because they show the behaviour instead of summarising it. Pick examples that differ along the dimension that causes trouble; three easy cases teach less than one easy case and two awkward ones.",
    { h: "Tests as the specification" },
    "Test-driven iteration applies the same idea to code. Write the test set before the implementation — the expected behaviour, the edge cases with sample inputs and expected outputs, and any performance requirement — then let Claude implement against it and iterate on the failures. A failing test is feedback with no ambiguity in it: this input, this expected output, this actual output. \"It's still not quite right\" gives Claude nothing to converge on, which is why iterations without tests tend to wander.",
    { h: "Let Claude ask first" },
    "Some requirements are not known to anyone until someone asks. In the interview pattern, Claude asks clarifying questions before implementing. For a caching layer that means TTL or event-based invalidation, whether stale data is acceptable when the cache is down, per-user or global caching, and the expected volume. These are design decisions with consequences that are easy to skip past in a one-line request. The pattern pays most in an unfamiliar domain, when a task has non-obvious implications, or when several approaches are viable and the right one depends on context only you have.",
    { h: "Grouping feedback" },
    "How you batch corrections affects how quickly the result converges. Issues that interact belong in one message: if fixing one changes the right fix for another, sending them one at a time invites a fix that collides with something you have not mentioned yet, and then a second round to undo it. Issues that are independent go one at a time, so each change is small, easy to verify and cannot be confused with the others.",
    { example: "You review a generated paginated endpoint and find three problems: the cursor encodes the wrong offset, the maximum page size is ignored, and a docstring has a typo. Sending all three as one list feels efficient, and sending them strictly one per message feels careful, but neither fits. The cursor and the page size are interdependent — the offset a cursor encodes depends on how many items a page holds — so they go in one message, ideally with two or three example requests and the exact cursor and items each should return. The docstring typo is independent and goes separately, where it cannot get tangled with the pagination fix." }
  ],

  "3.6": [
    { h: "Why the default invocation hangs" },
    "Run without flags, <code>claude</code> starts an interactive session and waits for a person. In a pipeline there is no person, so the job sits until the runner's timeout kills it. <code>-p</code> (or <code>--print</code>) is non-interactive mode, formerly called headless mode: it processes the one prompt, writes the result to standard output and exits. Options built around an environment variable or a <code>--batch</code> flag are invented, and redirecting standard input is a shell workaround for a problem the flag already solves.",
    { h: "Output a machine can use" },
    "Prose findings have to be copied into pull request comments by hand. <code>--output-format json</code> makes the output JSON, and <code>--json-schema</code> validates it against a schema you define — each finding with a file path, a line, a severity and a suggested fix — so the next pipeline step can parse the result and post inline comments through your repository host's API. The flags constrain the shape of the output; a prompt asking nicely for JSON only requests it.",
    { h: "Context the pipeline run needs" },
    "A CI run loads the project's CLAUDE.md like any other session, which makes that file the place for what the pipeline should know: testing standards, review criteria, the fixtures and factories that already exist. When generated tests hardcode data the project has factories for, the usual cause is that nobody documented the factories. Claude also cannot avoid duplicating tests it has never seen, so put the existing test file for a module in context when generating new ones; that removes the duplicates and keeps the new tests in the same style.",
    { h: "Review from an independent instance" },
    "The session that wrote the code is a poor reviewer of it. It still holds the reasoning that produced each decision, so it tends to re-confirm that reasoning rather than question it, and reviews come back shallow. An independent instance with no generation context sees only the diff and judges it on its merits — the same separation that multi-pass review relies on (see 4.6). When a review re-runs after new commits, give it the previous findings and ask only for issues that are new or still unresolved. Otherwise it re-reports problems that have already been fixed, and deciding whether an old finding still applies is a judgement about the current code that only the model, given the old findings, can make.",
    { example: "A nightly job runs <code>claude -p</code> to generate tests for changed modules. The tests run, but many duplicate scenarios the existing suite already covers, and most hardcode fixture data although the repository has a set of data factories. The tempting responses are a stronger model, or a longer prompt listing patterns to avoid. Neither addresses the cause: both problems are gaps in context. Claude has never seen the existing tests, so it cannot avoid repeating them, and nothing tells it the factories exist. Document the testing standards and the available factories in the project CLAUDE.md, which the CI run loads, and include each module's existing test file in the context of the generation step. Duplication falls and the new tests use the project's own fixtures." }
  ],

  "4.1": [
    { h: "Why vague instructions drift" },
    "An instruction such as <i>check comments for accuracy</i> does not describe a decision. It names a topic and leaves the model to invent the decision rule on every call. One run flags a TODO marker, the next flags a terse but correct docstring, the next misses a comment describing behaviour the code no longer has. None of these runs is wrong by the instruction's standard, which is exactly the problem: the instruction admits all of them.",
    "Adjectives fail for the same reason. <i>Be conservative</i> or <i>report only high-confidence findings</i> tells the model how cautious to feel, not where the line is. There is no shared threshold for conservative, so the volume of output shifts without the output becoming more consistent in kind. A categorical criterion — flag only when the comment contradicts the code, names something that does not exist, or is a TODO for a bug already fixed — can be checked against the code itself, so two runs reach the same answer.",
    { h: "Writing a criterion that holds" },
    "A usable criterion has three parts. The <b>inclusion rule</b> says what counts, in terms the model can verify from the input. The <b>exclusion list</b> says what looks similar but does not count, and this is where most false positives come from — stylistic staleness and minor wording are the near-misses a model will flag if nobody tells it not to. The <b>boundary examples</b> show one case on each side of the line, and they matter most on graded scales such as severity, where the difference between HIGH and MEDIUM is invisible until each level is anchored to a concrete failure.",
    "Anchor severity to consequence, not to the kind of code. <i>Null handling is critical</i> produces inconsistent ratings, because one null risk crashes a payment path and another sits in a log formatter. <i>CRITICAL means a runtime failure a user will hit</i> lets the model rate each instance by what it does. The same issue type can then legitimately land at different levels, and for a reason a developer can follow.",
    { h: "False positives are a property of the whole tool" },
    "Developers do not keep a mental table of which category is reliable. Once a noticeable share of findings is wrong, they start dismissing all of them, and the accurate security findings are ignored along with the noisy style ones. A category with a poor false-positive rate therefore costs far more than its own findings: it spends the credibility of every other category.",
    "That is why temporarily disabling a noisy category is a sound engineering move rather than a retreat. It stops the erosion immediately, keeps the high-precision categories delivering value, and gives you room to rewrite that category's criteria and re-enable it once it measures well. The alternatives the exam offers — a uniform strictness reduction, keeping everything on while improving over several weeks, or attaching confidence scores and letting developers sort it out — either leave the noise running or blunt the categories that already worked.",
    { h: "How the exam frames it" },
    "Questions describe a symptom — inconsistent flags, drifting severity, developers ignoring the tool — and offer fixes at different layers. The criteria answer wins when the root cause is an under-specified decision. The distractors add data (git blame, more surrounding code), add confidence thresholds, pre-filter the input, or change the model. None of them tells the model where the line is.",
    { example: "A triage prompt says <i>mark tickets urgent if they need fast attention</i>. The same kind of outage report is marked urgent on Monday and normal on Tuesday, and support agents have stopped trusting the flag. The tempting fix is to append <i>be careful to reserve urgent for truly urgent cases</i>, but that is another adjective: it lowers the urgent count without saying which tickets qualify. The right change states the rule — urgent only if a paying customer cannot complete a core action, data loss is reported, or a security exposure is described — lists what does not qualify, such as angrily worded feature requests or billing questions with no failure, and gives one example ticket per priority level. The decision now rests on facts in the ticket, so it repeats from run to run." }
  ],

  "4.2": [
    { h: "Why an example beats a description" },
    "A description of the behaviour you want is compressed. The writer knows what <i>actionable</i> or <i>precise</i> means and assumes the reader does too. An example is uncompressed: it shows the exact fields, their order, the level of detail and, crucially, the decision that was made. When instructions alone give output that is sometimes detailed and sometimes vague, adding more instructions tends to reproduce the variance, because each new sentence is one more thing to interpret. Two to four examples remove the interpretation step.",
    "The examples are not templates to be copied. The model infers the pattern behind them — what triggered the decision, what each field holds, how much to say — and applies it to inputs it has never seen. That makes choosing the examples the real work, because they define the pattern the model will extract.",
    { h: "Choosing the examples" },
    "Spend your examples where the model actually goes wrong. For ambiguous decisions, attach the rationale to the action; the rationale is the part that generalises, because it names the feature of the input that drove the choice. <i>Get me a manager</i> escalates because the customer explicitly asked for a human, not because the words sound frustrated. Without the rationale, the model may learn to escalate on tone.",
    "Make the set diverse and include at least one edge case. Examples that are all easy teach the model that hard cases do not occur, and it will then force a hard input into the easy shape. Keep the format identical across examples and identical to the output you want, since inconsistency between examples is itself a pattern the model will reproduce. For review tasks, pair an acceptable pattern with a genuine problem so the model learns the boundary rather than just the category.",
    { h: "Few-shot in extraction" },
    "Extraction is where few-shot earns the most. Documents vary in structure — a figure cited inline in one, pointed to a bibliography entry in another — and rules written for one layout miss the other. Informal units are the extreme case: <i>two handfuls</i>, <i>a pinch</i>, <i>a couple of boxes</i> are too varied to enumerate as rules, but a few examples that convert them to an approximate value while preserving the original text show the model what to do with the next phrasing it meets.",
    "Examples also reduce hallucination when one of them shows the value missing and the correct output as null. That demonstrates that <i>not found</i> is an acceptable answer, which a list of instructions rarely conveys as convincingly. Few-shot works alongside the rest of this domain rather than replacing it: criteria (see 4.1) say where the line is, examples show what each side looks like, and a schema (see 4.3) fixes the syntax. When output stays inconsistent in format or judgement despite clear instructions, examples are the answer the exam expects.",
    { example: "A procurement assistant extracts quantities from supplier emails into <code>quantity</code>, <code>unit</code>, <code>original_text</code> and <code>precision</code>. The prompt already holds a long list of unit rules, yet phrases like <i>a couple of pallets</i> or <i>same as last month</i> come back as invented exact numbers. The tempting fix is to extend the rule list, but the phrasings are open-ended and the list will never be complete. The better change is three examples: a standard quantity; an informal one mapped to an approximate value with <code>precision</code> set to approximate and the original words kept; and one that refers to a previous order not supplied, correctly returning a null quantity. The model now has a pattern for the informal case and explicit permission for the absent one." }
  ],

  "4.3": [
    { h: "What the schema actually buys" },
    "When the output is defined as a tool, the answer arrives as the input of a <code>tool_use</code> block, shaped by the tool's JSON schema, and you read the data from that block rather than scraping it out of text. That removes a whole class of failure — missing braces, trailing commas, a friendly sentence before the JSON — and ensures the required fields are present. What the schema cannot check is the values. An <code>invoice_total</code> that is a well-formed number and the wrong number passes every structural check; that boundary is where this lesson hands over to 4.4.",
    "Even the structural guarantee has edges. A refusal takes precedence over the schema, and a response that hits <code>max_tokens</code> stops mid-structure. Check <code>stop_reason</code> before trusting the payload, and keep your own validation on what you parse.",
    { h: "tool_choice decides whether you get structure at all" },
    "Defining a tool does not oblige the model to use it. With <code>tool_choice</code> on <code>auto</code>, the model may judge that a text answer is appropriate, and your parser receives prose. <code>any</code> requires a call to some tool: the setting for guaranteed structured output when several extraction schemas exist, because the model still picks the one that fits the input. Forcing a named tool goes further and fixes which tool runs, which is how you guarantee an order of operations such as <code>extract_metadata</code> before any enrichment.",
    { code: "tool_choice = {\"type\": \"auto\"}                             # may answer in text\ntool_choice = {\"type\": \"any\"}                              # must call one of the tools\ntool_choice = {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_metadata\"}  # must call this one", label: "The three tool_choice settings" },
    "The decision rule: <code>auto</code> when a text reply is a legitimate outcome, <code>any</code> when you need structure but the right schema depends on the input, a forced tool when one specific call must happen first. Forcing a single tool when inputs vary is the usual over-correction — it guarantees structure by making the model fit every document into one schema.",
    { h: "Designing a schema that cannot force a lie" },
    "A schema is also an instruction, and a badly designed one instructs the model to fabricate. A required field says <i>this always has a value</i>; when the source is silent, the model resolves the conflict by producing something plausible. Requiring only what is always present, and making the rest nullable, gives it an honest way out. Enums have the same problem at the category level. Without <code>other</code> an unusual item is forced into the nearest bucket, and without <code>unclear</code> the model must guess when the evidence supports no choice. Pairing <code>other</code> with a detail string keeps the information instead of flattening it.",
    "Normalisation rules belong in the prompt, because the schema can say a date is a string but not which format it takes. Without ISO 8601 dates, an amount plus a currency code and percentages as decimal fractions, two schema-valid outputs for the same document can disagree — a semantic error the schema accepts without complaint.",
    { example: "An intake pipeline handles invoices and receipts with two tools, <code>extract_invoice</code> and <code>extract_receipt</code>, and <code>tool_choice</code> left on <code>auto</code>. Now and then a response is a prose summary, which breaks the parser. One proposal is to force <code>extract_invoice</code>. It would end the prose, but every receipt would be squeezed into the invoice schema, and its required invoice number would be invented. Setting <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> guarantees a tool call while letting the model choose the schema that matches the document. If a later requirement says metadata must always be extracted first, that first step is where a forced tool belongs." }
  ],

  "4.4": [
    { h: "The loop, and why feedback must be specific" },
    "Validation sits between the model and everything downstream: nothing is written to a database or passed to another agent until it has passed two layers. Structural checks cover types, required fields and enum membership. Semantic checks are business rules — the line items sum to the total, the start date precedes the end date. With tool_use the structural layer rarely fails, so the semantic layer is where the real work is, because it catches what a schema cannot see.",
    "When a check fails, resending the same prompt mostly reproduces the same answer, since nothing about the model's input has changed. The retry has to change the input: the original document, the extraction it produced, and the concrete error. <i>The total is wrong</i> invites a guess; <i>total = 150 but the line items sum to 145</i> tells the model exactly which values to re-read. Including the previous extraction lets the model correct a specific value rather than start again and introduce new errors elsewhere.",
    { code: "<document>\n...original invoice text...\n</document>\n\nYour previous extraction:\n{\"total\": 150, \"line_items\": [...]}\n\nValidation failed: total = 150 but sum(line_items) = 145.\nRe-check the total and each line item against the document\nand return a corrected extraction.", label: "Retry prompt: document, previous output, specific error" },
    { h: "Knowing when a retry is pointless" },
    "Retry works when the answer is in the input and the model got it wrong: a date in the wrong format, a value in the wrong field, an arithmetic slip it can re-check. It does not work when the answer is not in the input. If the source never states the delivery date, or the figure that would reconcile the total lives in a schedule nobody supplied, every retry either fails again or — worse — invents a value that passes validation. The correct response is structural: return null, flag the record, obtain the missing document, or route it to a person.",
    "This is why a retry loop needs an exit. Cap the attempts, and when the cap is reached record why and hand the item off, rather than silently dropping it or accepting the last attempt. Raising the cap only spends more tokens on the same impossibility.",
    { h: "Building the checks into the output" },
    "Self-correction moves part of validation into the extraction itself. Asking for both <code>stated_total</code>, what the document says, and <code>calculated_total</code>, what the line items add up to, plus a <code>conflict_detected</code> flag, turns a silent inconsistency into a field your code can branch on. It also separates two different problems that a single <code>total</code> field conflates: the model misread the document, or the document itself is inconsistent.",
    "<code>detected_pattern</code> serves the longer feedback loop. When each finding records which rule or pattern triggered it, false positives can be grouped — if most rejected findings share one pattern, you fix that criterion or example instead of reviewing cases one at a time. In Python, Pydantic ties the loop together: one model generates the JSON Schema for the tool and runs the structural and custom validators on what comes back, so the schema and the checks cannot drift apart.",
    { example: "A contract pipeline extracts <code>start_date</code>, <code>end_date</code> and <code>renewal_fee</code>, and two failures recur. In the first, <code>end_date</code> precedes <code>start_date</code> because the model swapped them; a retry quoting both values and the violated rule fixes it on the next attempt. In the second, <code>renewal_fee</code> fails validation on every attempt, and the contract says only that the fee is <i>as set out in Schedule C</i>, which is not in the upload. The tempting response is to raise the retry limit. That cannot work: the information is not in the input, so more attempts either fail or fabricate. The right design makes <code>renewal_fee</code> nullable, records that it points to an external document, and routes the contract so the schedule can be supplied." }
  ],

  "4.5": [
    { h: "What you give up for the discount" },
    "The Message Batches API accepts a set of requests, processes them asynchronously and bills them at half the synchronous price. The cost of the discount is control over timing: processing can take up to 24 hours and there is no latency SLA. Many batches finish much sooner, but you plan against the ceiling rather than the typical case, because nothing commits to the typical case. Requests still unfinished at the 24-hour mark expire.",
    "The second constraint is structural. Each request in a batch is one request and one response. If the model asks to call a tool, nothing inside the batch can execute it and return the result for the model to continue. Any workflow that depends on multi-turn tool calling — a reviewer that requests related files mid-analysis, an agent loop — does not fit, however much latency it could tolerate. Single-shot work such as classification, extraction and report generation over supplied content is the natural fit.",
    { h: "The decision rule" },
    "Ask one question: is anyone, or anything, blocked until the result arrives? A pre-merge check, an interactive review or a chat reply has a waiting consumer, so it stays synchronous whatever the saving. An overnight report, a weekly audit, nightly test generation or a backlog of ten thousand documents has a deadline measured in hours or days, so batch is the default. A fallback to synchronous when a batch runs long does not rescue a blocking task — by then the developer has already waited.",
    { h: "Correlation, failure and SLA arithmetic" },
    "Results are not guaranteed to come back in submission order, so every request carries a <code>custom_id</code> linking its result to the source item. That identifier is also the unit of recovery. Each request succeeds or fails on its own, so when 5 of 100 documents exceed the context limit, you find the five by custom_id, change the strategy for them — split the long documents into chunks — and resubmit only those. Re-running the whole batch pays twice for 95 correct results.",
    "SLA arithmetic follows from the 24-hour ceiling. If results must be ready within 30 hours of an item arriving, the item must be submitted within 6 hours of arrival. A once-a-day submission fails that, because an item arriving just after a submission waits almost a day before the 24-hour clock even starts. Submitting every 4 hours keeps the worst case at 4 plus 24, or 28 hours, inside the guarantee with some margin.",
    "A batch also magnifies a bad prompt. Iterate on a representative sample first, one change at a time against the same sample, and commit the full volume only once the sample's output passes validation. Otherwise a flaw costs you the whole batch and a day of waiting.",
    { example: "A team processes claims that arrive throughout the day, with a promise of extracted data within 36 hours of receipt. Someone proposes one nightly batch at 01:00 to save cost. Worst case: a claim received at 01:05 waits nearly 24 hours for the next submission, then up to 24 hours of processing — about 48 hours, well past the promise. The deadline minus the processing ceiling gives a 12-hour submission window, so a 12-hour cadence is the bare minimum with no margin, and something shorter, such as every 6 hours, is the safe design. The tempting alternative, moving everything to synchronous calls, meets the SLA but throws away the 50% saving on work nobody is waiting on in real time." }
  ],

  "4.6": [
    { h: "Why the author is a poor reviewer" },
    "A model reviewing its own output in the same session reviews it with the context that produced it — the reasoning, the assumptions, the edge cases it considered and dismissed. Having already concluded the approach is correct, it tends to re-derive that conclusion rather than challenge it. Asking it to critique its work, or giving it more time to think, does not change those inputs, so the same blind spots survive. The subtle issues, such as an optimisation that breaks an edge case or a cleanup that quietly changes behaviour, are exactly the ones rationalised during generation.",
    "An independent instance starts from the artefact alone: the diff, the code and the requirements, but not the generator's reasoning. It has nothing to defend, so it judges the change on its merits. This mirrors human peer review, and the design rule is the same — separate the generator from the reviewer, and do not pass the generator's justifications along with the code.",
    { h: "Why one big pass is shallow" },
    "The second limit is volume. Given many files at once, attention spreads unevenly: some files get detailed feedback and others a single line, obvious bugs are missed, and identical code is flagged in one place and approved in another. A larger context window does not fix this — fitting the material in is not the same as attending to all of it evenly. Running the same whole-PR review several times and keeping only majority findings does not fix it either; it adds cost and suppresses real bugs that surfaced only once.",
    "The structural fix is a chain of focused passes. Each file gets its own local pass, which gives consistent depth. A separate integration pass then looks only at what crosses file boundaries: data flow between modules, type and contract consistency, circular dependencies. Neither pass can do the other's job. Local passes cannot see a mismatch between producer and consumer, and an integration pass that re-reads everything recreates the dilution. Because the steps are known in advance, this is prompt chaining rather than dynamic decomposition.",
    { h: "Routing with confidence" },
    "A final verification pass can rate its confidence in each finding and use it to route: high-confidence findings posted directly, low-confidence ones sent to a person or a further check. The caveat is calibration. Self-rated confidence is not a probability until it has been compared against labelled outcomes and thresholds set from that data (see 5.5); used raw as a hard filter, it discards real issues and passes bad ones with equal assurance. Putting the rationale and confidence in each finding also speeds up human triage without hiding anything.",
    { example: "A refactor renames <code>customer_id</code> to <code>account_id</code> in a producer module and updates most consumers across 18 files. The session that wrote the change is asked to review it and reports no issues. The team then runs one review pass over all 18 files on a model with a larger context window; it comments heavily on the first few files and misses that one consumer still reads the old field. The tempting next step is to repeat that pass with a stronger prompt. The better architecture has three parts: an independent reviewer with no access to the generation session, per-file passes for local correctness, and an integration pass that traces the renamed field from the producer to every consumer — the pass that actually catches the stale read. Low-confidence findings from verification go to a human, with thresholds calibrated on past reviews." }
  ],

  "5.1": [
    { h: "What actually fills the window" },
    "The Messages API is stateless: every request carries the whole conversation you want Claude to see — the system prompt, the tool definitions, every prior turn and every tool result. Nothing is remembered between calls, so what the model knows on turn thirty is exactly what your application chose to send on turn thirty. Context is therefore a budget your code spends on every call, not a store that fills up by itself.",
    "Because those four components compete for the same tokens, growth in one squeezes the others. Tool results are usually the worst offender: they arrive on every call, they are rarely removed, and most of their fields have nothing to do with the task. When the budget runs short, applications typically start summarising older history — and that is where the more dangerous failure begins.",
    { h: "Why summaries lose the facts you need" },
    "A summariser optimises for gist. Asked to compress twenty turns, it keeps the story — the customer was unhappy, a discount was discussed — and drops the specifics that made the story actionable: the percentage, the order number, the date the item arrived. Each later round works from the previous summary, so precision decays progressively and cannot be recovered. Telling the summariser to keep every number verbatim is weaker than it looks: it is still a probabilistic instruction applied to a growing history, and one paraphrased figure is enough to issue the wrong refund.",
    "The robust fix separates two kinds of information. Narrative can be summarised. Transactional facts — identifiers, amounts, dates, what the customer asked for, the current status — are extracted into the case-facts block as soon as they appear, updated when they change, and rebuilt into every prompt outside the summarised history. The summary can then become as vague as it likes, because nothing a tool call or a refund depends on lives there. Raising the summarisation threshold only delays the loss, and storing the transcript for retrieval adds a system that still has to guess when to look something up.",
    { h: "Position and volume" },
    "Even without summarisation, a long input is not read evenly. The beginning and the end are processed reliably; a finding in the middle is the one most likely to be missed, even when it directly answers the question. So when you assemble aggregated input for a downstream step — a synthesis agent reading several subagents' output, for instance — put a key-findings summary at the top, organise the detail under explicit section headings so the middle is navigable, and close with the action items. Cutting everything down to fit a smaller window, or rotating which source goes first, treats the symptom and throws detail away.",
    "Volume is best controlled at the source. A tool that returns forty fields when five matter should be trimmed before its result enters the context, and a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook does that deterministically on every call instead of trusting the model to ignore the noise (the hook itself is covered in 1.5). The same logic applies to subagents: ask for structured output — key facts, quotes, dates and sources as fields — rather than page content and reasoning traces. Metadata that exists only inside prose is the first thing a later summary drops; metadata in a named field survives.",
    { example: "A support agent handles long conversations that often cover several issues. Logs show that deep into a conversation it quotes the wrong refund amount: the figure was stated early and now survives in the compressed history only as “a partial refund was agreed”. One option rewrites the summarisation prompt to preserve every number, date and amount verbatim; another extracts amounts, dates and order IDs into a persistent case-facts block included in every prompt. The case-facts block is right because it removes the dependency on the summary altogether — the figure is present on every turn no matter how many compressions have happened. The prompt rewrite is tempting because it aims straight at the visible symptom, but it leaves critical facts inside a lossy process and fails again the next time the summariser paraphrases." }
  ],

  "5.2": [
    { h: "Criteria, not proxies" },
    "Escalation is a decision boundary, and an agent can only respect a boundary it has been shown. The reliable triggers share one property: each is an observable fact about the request, not an inference about how hard it feels. The customer asked for a human. The policy does not cover what they want. The agent has tried and is not making progress. The amount is over a threshold. You can write each one down, illustrate it and audit whether the agent followed it.",
    "The unreliable triggers are all inferences. Sentiment measures mood, and an angry customer with a routine damaged-item claim is still a routine case, while a perfectly calm customer asking for a policy exception is not. Self-rated confidence fails because the model's sense of its own certainty is poorly calibrated — it can be confidently wrong, which is precisely the case escalation exists to catch. A classifier trained on historical tickets is heavy machinery that needs labelled data you may not have, and it adds a component before the simple fix has been tried.",
    { h: "Fixing miscalibrated escalation" },
    "When an agent escalates easy cases and handles policy exceptions on its own, the root cause is an unclear boundary, and the proportionate first fix is explicit escalation criteria in the system prompt with few-shot examples on both sides of the line: a standard replacement for a damaged item with photo evidence, resolved autonomously; a competitor price-match request, escalated. Examples show the model where the line falls in a way adjectives such as “complex” never do. Hard limits are different in kind. A financial threshold must hold every time, so it is enforced by a hook that intercepts the call, not by a sentence in the prompt (see 1.4 and 1.5).",
    "The policy-gap trigger needs care. The test is whether policy is silent or ambiguous for this specific request, not whether the conversation is uncomfortable. Tracking evidence that contradicts the customer, a customer who might change their mind, or a message that combines a billing question with a return are all within scope — the agent has the data and the policy to act. Price matching against a competitor when policy only covers drops on your own site is outside it, and the agent must not invent policy to fill the gap.",
    { h: "Ordering the response" },
    "An explicit request for a human is honoured at once: no further investigation, no “let me just look up your order first”. Anything else within scope gets a genuine attempt — acknowledge the frustration, offer a concrete resolution, and escalate if the customer then reiterates that they want a person. Identity ambiguity is a separate case with its own rule. When a lookup returns several customers, the agent asks for another identifier such as an email, phone number or order number. Picking the account with the most recent order, or making the tool return a single “best” match, turns a one-turn clarification into a silent action on someone else's account. When escalation does happen, the human sees only the structured handoff, so it must stand on its own (see 1.4).",
    { example: "A customer writes: “This is the second broken blender you've sent me and I'm furious.” The order is found, the damage is covered, and policy allows a replacement or refund. One option escalates because sentiment analysis scores the message as strongly negative; another escalates because the model rates its own confidence at 6 out of 10. Neither is a valid trigger. The right response acknowledges the frustration, offers the replacement or refund that policy allows, and escalates only if the customer then asks for a person. The sentiment option is tempting because the customer is plainly upset, but anger is not complexity and this case is squarely within the agent's scope. Had the first message been “put me through to a manager”, the answer would flip: escalate immediately, without offering the replacement first." }
  ],

  "5.3": [
    { h: "What the coordinator has to decide" },
    "A coordinator facing a subagent failure has a small set of moves: retry with a modified query, use the partial results, hand the work to a different subagent or source, or carry on without that section and annotate the gap. It can only choose well if the failure report says which moves are open. Each field in the structured error maps to one of those decisions — the failure type says whether a retry is worth it, the attempted query says what to modify, the partial results say what is already usable, the alternatives say where else to look, and the coverage impact says what the final report will be missing.",
    "A generic “search unavailable” collapses all of that into a single bit. The coordinator cannot tell a timeout from a permission failure, cannot reuse the results that did come back, and ends up either retrying blindly or giving up on the section. The same logic applies to error categories more broadly: transient failures such as timeouts, 503s and network errors are retryable; validation errors need the input changed; business errors such as a policy violation need an explanation and an alternative; permission errors need escalation. Only the first belongs in a retry loop.",
    { h: "Failure is not the same as empty" },
    "The distinction the exam probes hardest is between an access failure and a valid empty result. A timeout means the question was never answered. “0 results” means it was answered and the answer is nothing — which is a finding in its own right, since the absence of reports on a topic tells the coordinator something. Reporting both as failures wastes a retry on a good answer. Reporting a timeout as an empty success is silent suppression, and it is worse: the coordinator and the eventual reader both conclude the evidence does not exist when it was never checked.",
    { h: "Where recovery belongs" },
    "Handle an error at the lowest level that can resolve it. A subagent should retry a transient failure once or twice itself — the coordinator gains nothing from hearing about a blip that cleared — and then propagate what remains along with its partial results, rather than retrying indefinitely and burning latency. At the opposite extreme, aborting the whole workflow because one source failed discards every piece of work that succeeded. The middle path is graceful degradation: keep what completed, and make the gaps visible in the synthesis by marking each section FULL COVERAGE or PARTIAL COVERAGE with the reason, so the reader can judge how far each conclusion can be trusted.",
    "Coverage annotations also protect against a quieter failure. A synthesis agent given incomplete input will still write fluent, confident prose; without an annotation, nothing in the finished report distinguishes a well-supported section from one built on half the evidence.",
    { example: "A research subagent searches three source types. Academic databases return fifteen relevant papers, the industry-report search returns no matches, and the patent database times out. One option reports a single “67% source coverage” figure with detailed logs available on request; another reports both the empty search and the timeout as failures needing intervention. The right design reports the patent search as an access failure with the attempted query and an alternative approach, reports the industry-report search as a successful query that found nothing, and passes the fifteen papers up as results. The percentage is tempting because it looks like structured reporting, but it hides which source failed and why — exactly what the coordinator needs to decide to retry the patents and accept the empty industry result." }
  ],

  "5.4": [
    { h: "What degradation looks like" },
    "A long investigation fills the window with file contents, search output and exploratory dead ends. As it fills, the specific details read early — which class a processor inherits from, the three places a method is called — become harder for the model to use, and its answers drift toward what code like this usually does. Hearing about “typical patterns”, or getting generic advice where you expected named classes and files, is the tell. The remedy is not to reread everything; it is to structure the work so that the details that matter never depend on one long, noisy history.",
    { h: "Isolate discovery, hand over conclusions" },
    "Delegation is the first lever. An Explore subagent reads the files and traces the imports in its own context, then returns a line such as “payments depends on AuthService, OrderModel and the external gateway API”. The main agent keeps coordination, design and implementation — the phases where retained context is most valuable — and pays for discovery once, in a window that is then thrown away. Give each subagent a constrained budget: the specific question, only the data it needs, a restricted <code>allowedTools</code> set so it has fewer ways to wander, and an instruction to return structured findings rather than raw dumps.",
    "Between phases, summarise. The next subagent should start from conclusions — the list of call sites, the chosen pattern, the open questions — not from the transcript of how they were found. In a multi-agent system the coordinator is its own context layer: it aggregates what workers return, holds global state and decides what each worker receives, which stops one agent's verbose output leaking into everyone else's window.",
    { h: "Make findings outlive the window" },
    "A scratchpad file is memory that sits outside the context. Writing key findings to it as they are established — inheritance relationships, call sites, external rate limits, the migration that added a required column — means they survive compaction, a context reset or a fresh session, and the agent can consult the file instead of rerunning discovery. <code>/compact</code> is the other lever, useful when verbose tool output has filled the window, but it summarises, so it carries the same risk as any summary in 5.1: exact figures and dates blur. Get the precise facts into the scratchpad before compacting, not after. Resuming an old session has its own trap — the files may have changed since (see 1.7).",
    "For multi-agent runs the same idea becomes crash recovery. Each agent exports its state to a known location — status, queries executed, key findings, coverage and gaps — and the coordinator keeps a manifest of which agents have completed, which are in progress and which have not started. After a crash it reads the manifest and resumes the unfinished phase instead of repeating work that already succeeded.",
    { example: "You are adding error-handling wrappers around external API calls across a large codebase in three phases: find every call site, agree the wrapper design with the developer, then apply it consistently. The discovery phase lists hundreds of call sites with surrounding code and fills the window before it finishes. Options include staying in the main conversation and running /compact periodically, splitting the work across several sessions that share a CLAUDE.md, or running discovery in an Explore subagent that returns a summary while design and implementation continue in the main conversation. The subagent is right: it isolates the verbose phase and preserves the main window for the phases where consistency depends on retained context. Repeated compaction is tempting because it is built in and cheap, but it summarises the very call-site detail the implementation phase needs, and leaves discovery competing with design for the same window." }
  ],

  "5.5": [
    { h: "Why the aggregate misleads" },
    "An overall accuracy figure is an average weighted by volume. A document type that is a small share of traffic can fail badly and barely move it, which is how 97% overall coexists with 40% errors on one type or one field. The average also hides where the damage lands: a wrong invoice total costs far more than a wrong formatting detail. So the unit of validation is the segment — accuracy by document type and by field, each measured separately — and it is established before anyone decides to automate.",
    "Automation is then granted per segment, not switched on for the whole pipeline. Where a document type and field have shown stable accuracy, extractions can flow straight through; where accuracy is unproven, unstable or poor, they stay under review. That is a more granular decision than “keep human review” or “remove it”, and it is the one the exam rewards.",
    { h: "Making confidence scores mean something" },
    "Field-level confidence lets you route individual fields rather than whole documents. But a raw score is only a number the model produced; until you know what error rate it corresponds to, a threshold of 0.9 is arbitrary. Calibration supplies that meaning. Run the pipeline over a labelled validation set, see how measured accuracy varies with the reported score, and set the review threshold where the error rate becomes acceptable for that field. Choosing a threshold to match the review team's capacity, or picking a round number, sets it by convenience rather than by risk.",
    "This is why self-rated confidence is rejected as an escalation trigger in 5.2 yet accepted here. In the support case the model's score is taken at face value. In extraction the score is checked against ground truth, and the threshold is derived from that check.",
    { h: "Auditing the part you trust" },
    "Routing low-confidence and ambiguous-source extractions to humans covers the cases the model knows it is unsure about. It does nothing for the cases where it is confidently wrong, and that is where new error patterns tend to appear — a supplier changes its layout, a new document type starts arriving. Stratified random sampling closes the gap: draw a regular random sample from each stratum, including the high-confidence band and each document type, and review it against ground truth. That measures the real error rate inside each band and surfaces drift before it has been applied to thousands of documents. Reviewing only the low-confidence queue cannot tell you this, and nor can waiting for downstream complaints, which only reveal errors someone happened to notice.",
    { example: "An invoice-extraction pipeline has run for three months at 97% overall accuracy, and most fields carry high confidence scores. The team proposes removing human review entirely. One option objects that 97% is not high enough; another says accuracy should first be broken down by document type and field. The second is right: if, say, scanned receipts or line-item totals are far below the average, those segments stay under review while the stable ones are automated, and a stratified sample of high-confidence output keeps being audited. The “not high enough” objection is tempting because it sounds cautious, but no single overall threshold answers the question — the aggregate is the very thing hiding the problem." }
  ],

  "5.6": [
    { h: "Where attribution is lost" },
    "A subagent reading a report knows exactly where each figure came from. The link breaks when that knowledge is written as prose — “the AI music market is estimated at $3.2B” — and is then summarised, merged with other agents' output and summarised again. Nothing fails loudly. The final report simply contains a claim nobody can trace, with no date to say whether it is still current. The defence is structural: every claim travels as a record carrying its source URL, source name, publication date, a supporting quote where useful, and a confidence value.",
    "Aggregation is the step to watch because that is where the pressure to compress is strongest. A synthesis agent asked for a readable report will drop citations unless its output contract requires them, so the requirement to carry claim → source records through has to be stated for every step that combines results, not only for the subagents that first find them.",
    { h: "Conflicts are findings" },
    "When two credible sources disagree, the disagreement is itself information about the evidence. Picking the more plausible figure discards half of it and makes the report look more certain than it is; a footnote is the same choice with a disclaimer attached; listing both without marking them lets a later agent choose silently. The correct record keeps both values, each with its source, date and, where known, methodology, sets an explicit conflict flag, and may suggest an explanation such as a different method or period.",
    "Who resolves the conflict is a question of responsibilities. The analysis agent sees one slice of the evidence, so it completes its own work with the conflict annotated and passes it up — it neither blocks the pipeline waiting for a ruling nor decides unilaterally. The coordinator, which sees every source and the research question, reconciles before synthesis. The finished report then separates stable findings from disputed ones, so a reader knows which conclusions rest on agreement and which on a judgement call.",
    { h: "Dates and presentation" },
    "Dates turn apparent contradictions into trends. Two figures a year apart can both be correct, and without publication dates a synthesis step will present growth as a dispute. Requiring a date on every claim costs almost nothing and removes a whole class of false conflicts.",
    "Presentation is the last place provenance can be lost. Render by content type rather than forcing one format onto everything: financial figures in tables where values and sources can be compared side by side, news and analysis as prose, technical findings as structured lists, time series in chronological order.",
    { example: "A research pipeline finds that one source puts AI-generated music at 12% of streaming, based on automated classification, while a survey of labels published a few months later puts it at 8%. The document-analysis subagent has both, with dates and methods. Options: treat the larger source as authoritative and footnote the other; average the two; stop and ask the coordinator which to trust before continuing; or complete the analysis with both values attributed, flag the conflict with the methodological difference noted, and let the coordinator reconcile. The last is right. Stopping is tempting because it looks cautious, but it stalls the pipeline for a decision that belongs to the coordinator anyway and can be made just as well once the analysis is finished — by the agent with the wider view of the evidence." }
  ]

};
